1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,243
In the 1520s, a Portuguese
priest reached the Ethiopian

2
00:00:05,243 --> 00:00:09,703
highlands and found churches
cut down into solid rock.

3
00:00:10,153 --> 00:00:12,914
Neither a jeweller in
silver, he wrote, nor a

4
00:00:12,914 --> 00:00:15,123
worker of wax in wax, could do more.

5
00:00:15,573 --> 00:00:19,221
Then he stopped writing. He was sure
no one at home would believe him.

6
00:00:19,671 --> 00:00:22,079
So he swore an oath: I swear by God that

7
00:00:22,079 --> 00:00:24,066
all that is written is the truth.

8
00:00:24,516 --> 00:00:27,865
What did Francisco Alvares
see? Follow for Part 2.

9
00:00:29,815 --> 00:00:33,380
Eleven churches, in a town
once called Roha. Not built

10
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:36,087
up from the ground, but cut down into it.

11
00:00:36,537 --> 00:00:40,456
Each one is a single block
left standing in a pit. Roof,

12
00:00:40,456 --> 00:00:43,535
columns, even the altars
are the same stone.

13
00:00:43,985 --> 00:00:46,619
Saint George's church sits in a pit about

14
00:00:46,619 --> 00:00:49,190
eleven metres deep. Its roof is a cross.

15
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:52,107
And some of its lower
windows are false. They

16
00:00:52,107 --> 00:00:53,971
do not go through the wall at all.

17
00:00:54,421 --> 00:00:57,486
Alvares paced out another
church: a hundred and

18
00:00:57,486 --> 00:01:00,095
twenty spans long. Its name is Golgotha.

19
00:01:00,545 --> 00:01:04,279
Why would an Ethiopian town have
a Golgotha? Comment your guess.

20
00:01:06,229 --> 00:01:08,809
Did you know? Lalibela has its own river

21
00:01:08,809 --> 00:01:11,519
Jordan: a seasonal stream called Yordanos.

22
00:01:11,969 --> 00:01:15,389
Nearby are a Mount Sinai, a
Bethlehem, a Tomb of Adam,

23
00:01:15,389 --> 00:01:17,985
and a hill named for the Mount of Olives.

24
00:01:18,435 --> 00:01:22,147
Tradition says the king was told
to build a second Jerusalem.

25
00:01:22,597 --> 00:01:26,412
A popular theory: Saladin
took Jerusalem in

26
00:01:26,412 --> 00:01:29,872
1187, and the pilgrims' road grew hard.

27
00:01:30,322 --> 00:01:32,785
Other scholars doubt he meant to replace

28
00:01:32,785 --> 00:01:35,186
Jerusalem at all. So who was this king?

29
00:01:37,136 --> 00:01:39,881
Gebre Meskel Lalibela, a king of the Zagwe

30
00:01:39,881 --> 00:01:42,299
line, around the year twelve hundred.

31
00:01:42,749 --> 00:01:45,909
Alvares was told about his
birth: a swarm of bees

32
00:01:45,909 --> 00:01:48,359
covered the baby, and did him no harm.

33
00:01:48,809 --> 00:01:52,261
One old reading of his name:
the bees have acknowledged

34
00:01:52,261 --> 00:01:55,273
his rule. Alvares heard
it more simply: miracle.

35
00:01:55,723 --> 00:01:58,421
His Life says his brother
feared him, and that God

36
00:01:58,421 --> 00:02:00,417
showed him in a vision what to build.

37
00:02:00,867 --> 00:02:04,792
And then comes the strangest claim
of all. Who worked the night shift?

38
00:02:06,742 --> 00:02:09,698
Church tradition says
men cut the rock by day.

39
00:02:09,698 --> 00:02:12,075
By night, angels carried on the work.

40
00:02:12,525 --> 00:02:15,006
Alvares heard that it all took twenty‑four

41
00:02:15,006 --> 00:02:17,368
years. And he was told: this is written.

42
00:02:17,818 --> 00:02:21,726
The king's Life survives in
manuscripts from the fourteenth or

43
00:02:21,726 --> 00:02:24,815
fifteenth century, kept
in monasteries far apart.

44
00:02:25,265 --> 00:02:27,811
So by then, Ethiopians far from Roha

45
00:02:27,811 --> 00:02:30,428
already honoured Lalibela as a saint.

46
00:02:30,878 --> 00:02:33,865
But what do the archaeologists
say? Save this one.

47
00:02:35,815 --> 00:02:39,071
In 2004, the Cambridge archaeologist David

48
00:02:39,071 --> 00:02:41,319
Phillipson made a bold claim.

49
00:02:41,769 --> 00:02:46,347
Two of the churches, Merkorios
and Gabriel‑Rufael, may have

50
00:02:46,347 --> 00:02:50,537
begun as a palace or a
fortress, around AD 600 to 800.

51
00:02:50,987 --> 00:02:54,806
In his view, the site grew over
five or six centuries, and

52
00:02:54,806 --> 00:02:57,835
Lalibela's reign was its
last, greatest phase.

53
00:02:58,285 --> 00:03:01,497
Other scholars, Jacques Mercier
and Claude Lepage, keep

54
00:03:01,497 --> 00:03:04,066
the old date: one king,
a couple of decades.

55
00:03:04,516 --> 00:03:07,753
Nobody yet knows exactly
when each church was cut.

56
00:03:07,753 --> 00:03:10,148
And one old theory should be retired.

57
00:03:12,098 --> 00:03:16,282
Templars, Byzantines, Indians,
Armenians: over the years,

58
00:03:16,282 --> 00:03:19,586
people credited almost
anyone but Ethiopians.

59
00:03:20,036 --> 00:03:23,275
Recent studies favour local
builders. The windows

60
00:03:23,275 --> 00:03:26,052
and columns follow the old style of Aksum.

61
00:03:26,502 --> 00:03:30,862
In the 1520s, pilgrims put
their hands into a hole in a

62
00:03:30,862 --> 00:03:34,587
stone in Golgotha's floor,
asking for miracles.

63
00:03:35,037 --> 00:03:37,991
They still come. At Genna
and Timkat, crowds in

64
00:03:37,991 --> 00:03:40,819
white fill the courtyards
and the rims above.

65
00:03:41,269 --> 00:03:43,061
So what should we make of the wonder?

66
00:03:45,011 --> 00:03:50,199
Look at what we traced: an eyewitness
from the 1520s, a king's Life

67
00:03:50,199 --> 00:03:54,845
copied for centuries, and
archaeologists who still disagree.

68
00:03:55,295 --> 00:03:58,043
When a post says aliens
or Templars did it, ask

69
00:03:58,043 --> 00:04:00,031
who first wrote it down, and when.

70
00:04:01,981 --> 00:04:06,013
Some want a wonder made overnight.
Some say none of it can be true.

71
00:04:06,463 --> 00:04:09,764
The stone tells a quieter
story: patient hands, day

72
00:04:09,764 --> 00:04:12,223
after day, offering their work to God.

73
00:04:12,673 --> 00:04:14,983
Choose one small task this week. Do it

74
00:04:14,983 --> 00:04:17,111
slowly, carefully, and with prayer.

75
00:04:19,061 --> 00:04:21,151
Thank you for sitting with us over coffee.

76
00:04:21,601 --> 00:04:23,073
This is Ethiopics.
